      "content": "Benchmarking the performance of a local coding agent model on the NVIDIA RTX 4090 requires a granular examination of hardware constraints, software optimizations, and the inherent trade-offs between latency, throughput, and output fidelity. The RTX 4090, with its 24GB of GDDR6X memory and approximately 284 TOPS of FP16 compute capability, represents the current apex of consumer-grade AI acceleration, yet it imposes strict boundaries on the size and complexity of models that can be deployed effectively for agentic workflows. The primary bottleneck in local deployment is rarely raw compute throughput for inference but rather memory bandwidth and capacity, which dictate the maximum context window, the precision of the weights, and the feasibility of speculative decoding techniques. When evaluating a coding agent, we are not merely measuring token generation speed but the end-to-end latency of a loop that involves prompt ingestion, reasoning, tool invocation, and code synthesis, all of which must occur within a timeframe that maintains developer productivity.\n\nThe first critical area of analysis is Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) speculative decoding, a technique designed to mitigate the sequential nature of autoregressive generation. In standard autoregressive models, each token must be generated before the next can be predicted, creating a linear dependency chain that limits throughput. MTP architectures, such as those found in Mistral Small or specialized variants of Llama 3, predict multiple future tokens in parallel during the forward pass. During inference, a smaller, faster draft model generates a sequence of candidate tokens, which are then verified in parallel by the larger target model. On an RTX 4090, the effectiveness of MTP is highly dependent on the acceptance rate of the draft tokens. If the draft model is too small or poorly aligned with the target model, the acceptance rate drops, and the overhead of verifying rejected tokens outweighs 